WEBVTT
TITLE Recursion — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
A recursive function solves a copy of its own problem on a smaller input, and has one input where it stops.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.000
sumTo(n) returns n + sumTo(n-1). The base case is n == 0, which returns 0 and calls nothing.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Two obligations: the argument must move towards the base case, and the base case must be on that path.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Everything else is bookkeeping: the machine must remember where it was, and what it had already computed.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Each call gets its own frame: its own parameters, its own locals, and its own place to return to.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
sumTo(3) pushes four frames — n = 3, 2, 1, 0 — before anything is added. Only the last one returns without calling.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Then the stack unwinds: 0, then 0+1 = 1, then 1+2 = 3, then 3+3 = 6. The deepest frame finishes first.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
So the peak depth is what the machine must hold at once, and the number of frames equals the number of calls.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
A linear recursion calls itself once per level. sumTo(n) and fact(n) both reach a peak depth of n+1 frames.

10
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.000
A branching recursion calls itself twice. Naive fib(n) makes two calls per level, so the calls multiply while the depth stays n+1.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.000
The page counts them: fib(10) makes 177 calls and never goes deeper than 10 frames. fib(30) makes 2,692,537 calls at depth 30.

12
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
GATE 2015 Set 2 is the same gap in miniature: fun(5) returns 51 after 81 calls, at a peak depth of 5 frames.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Remove the base case and the function keeps calling. In C that is not an endless loop: it consumes stack until the stack runs out.

14
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.000
The standard calls that undefined behaviour, not a required error. It needs no diagnostic, and the result is not yours to predict.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.000
A base case can exist and still be unreachable: f(n) = f(n-2) with a base case at n == 1 never stops for an even n.

16
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.000
So ask two separate questions: does the argument move towards the base case, and does the base case lie on that path?

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
sumTo can be written as a loop. Same arithmetic, no frames: constant extra space instead of space proportional to n.

18
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.000
A tail call is a call whose result is returned immediately, with no work left to do afterwards. Its frame is no longer needed.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Some compilers reuse that frame anyway. But the C standard does not require it, so you may not assume constant stack.

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
An accumulator makes the call a tail call; a loop removes the calls. Both keep the answer, and the page checks that they do.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
GATE CSE 2015 Set 2: fun(1) is 1; for any larger n, x starts at 1 and gains fun(k) * fun(n-k) for k = 1 to n-1.

22
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Work upward, not downward: fun(2) = 1 + 1·1 = 2, fun(3) = 1 + 1·2 + 2·1 = 5, fun(4) = 1 + 5 + 4 + 5 = 15.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:53.000
fun(5) = 1 + 1·15 + 2·5 + 5·2 + 15·1 = 51. The answer is 51, and the model agrees.

24
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The page also counts the calls: 1, 3, 9, 27, 81 — three to the power n-1. One mark, eighty-one calls, if you trace them all.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
GATE CSE 2021 Set 2: SomeFunction subtracts the smaller argument from the larger and calls itself again.

26
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:15.000
That is the Euclidean algorithm by repeated subtraction, so it returns the gcd. The page checks the identity on every pair up to 40.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:23.000
So SomeFunction(15, 255) is 15: the trace reaches (15, 15) after sixteen subtractions. The official key is 15.

28
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
GATE CSE 2011: foo(n, r) = n mod r + foo(n / r, r) adds the digits of n in base r. foo(513, 2) = 2.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
GATE CSE 2014 Set 2: f(x) returns x when |x*x - 3| < 0.01, and otherwise calls f(x/2 + 1.5/x).

30
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.000
That update is Newton's step for x*x - 3 = 0, so the sequence converges to the square root of 3, about 1.732.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:53.000
So the answer is a range, not one number: the official key accepts 1.72 to 1.74. Any q with |q*q - 3| < 0.01 returns itself at once.

32
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.000
One warning about this snippet as C: abs() takes an int, so abs(x*x - 3) is not the test written. The key answers the mathematical reading.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Every parameter and every automatic local is a fresh object in each frame. That is why one name can hold many values at once.

34
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
A static local is one object for the whole run. All frames read and write the same counter, so it counts calls, not depth.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
An array parameter is a pointer, so every frame sees the same array. Recursion can fill it in place, and all levels see the change.

36
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
So before any trace, sort every name in the function into per-frame or shared. The answer depends on that sort, not on the arithmetic.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.000
For a value question: write the call tree on paper, evaluate the base cases, then combine on the way up. Do not run it in one pass.

38
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:45.000
For a cost question: write the recurrence for the number of calls, then solve it. Calls and depth are two different recurrences.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Recursion is also the tool behind divide and conquer, tree traversals, and the recurrence-relations lesson you have already met.

40
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
The two errors that cost the most marks: assuming a static local is per frame, and assuming the call returns before the rest of the expression.

